21. How does data flow work in React?
React follows one-way data flow.
Parent → Child
Data is passed using props.
function Parent() {
  const name = "Ekta";

  return <Child name={name} />;
}

function Child({ name }) {
  return <h1>{name}</h1>;
}
Flow:
Parent
  ↓
props
  ↓
Child
Child → Parent
A child doesn't directly modify the parent's state.
Instead, the parent passes a function to the child.
function Parent() {
  const handleMessage = (message) => {
    console.log(message);
  };

  return <Child onSend={handleMessage} />;
}

function Child({ onSend }) {
  return (
    <button onClick={() => onSend("Hello Parent")}>
      Send
    </button>
  );
}
Flow:
Parent
  ↓ function
Child
  ↓ calls function
Parent state/function
Important interview concept
React's normal data flow is:
Parent
   ↓
Child
   ↓
Grandchild
For shared state:
          Store / Context
          ↙           ↘
      Component A   Component B
